  <has_title rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">UDI U818A Camera Assembly Replacement</has_title>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="#Step_130695">
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#Step"/>
  <has_tool rdf:resource="#Tool_ph000_phillips_screwdriver"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/ddnJvjZeU4TQcO2u.standard"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/tCuWPScQqAr4Cdll.standard"/>
  <has_title rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Using #PH000 screwdriver remove the 5mm screw retaining the propeller to the rotating post; there is one for each propeller. You can now safely pull the propeller away from its apparatus.</has_title>
  <has_order rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0</has_order>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="#Step_130677">
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#Step"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/HagM4fQ6osyZFiVI.standard"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/oba1QmKSkiKU5iuQ.standard"/>
  <has_title rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">After removing the propellers, unscrew all 4 (3x3mm) screws connecting the electronics to the frame. Once you removed all the screws holding the plastic dust cover to the frame, pull the 2 LED bars through the dust cover and remove the thin plastic dust cover from the drone. You can now access the piece connecting the electronics to the drone cover, but do not remove electronics until after disconnecting the drone head lamp first.</has_title>
  <has_order rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">1</has_order>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="#Step_130679">
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#Step"/>
  <has_tool rdf:resource="#Tool_ph000_phillips_screwdriver"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/tDVYZur2FdHeQGpi.standard"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/N5WIJIkUpATIwl3I.standard"/>
  <has_title rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Step two is disconnecting and removing the head lamp. Use a screw driver for 5mm screw to remove head lamp of drone. You can now disconnect the electronics from the frame of the drone by pulling each arm out of the hole in the frame holding it in place. Do not try to separate the frame from the electronics yet! First, check to see if there is tape holding the head lamp wires in place underneath the electronics.</has_title>
  <has_order rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2</has_order>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="#Step_130680">
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#Step"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/bvFhDdk5BitBtLLZ.standard"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/WX4uDflQivJaWgyD.standard"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/qxF4PKDAB3FKSiIh.standard"/>
  <has_tool rdf:resource="#Tool_metal_spudger_set"/>
  <has_title rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Lastly, if there is white tape over the black and red wires for the head lamp, remove the tape with a metal spudger. Remove this tape but try to keep it in reusable condition to re-secure the headlight later so there are no loose wires. Separate the plastic tabs securing the motor arms to the drone cover and delicately pull out the arm. Do this for all 4 arms to remove the frame from the cover.</has_title>
  <has_order rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">3</has_order>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="#Step_130691">
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#Step"/>
  <has_tool rdf:resource="#Tool_ph000_phillips_screwdriver"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/fYXYbLqAPPCpbr4F.standard"/>
  <has_title rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Using a #PH000  screwdriver to remove the 4 (5mm) corner screws that secure both the receiving board and the motor arm. Notice how  the wires tuck neatly in the bottom channel of the motor arm.</has_title>
  <has_order rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</has_order>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="#Step_130692">
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#Step"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/M3pwRtCxgWFtyPGo.standard"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/2kv3NtGAHWctBSHm.standard"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/vWN5uOYOWQfH2lrG.standard"/>
  <has_tool rdf:resource="#Tool_metal_spudger_set"/>
  <has_title rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Carefully slide the arm out from the socket. A metal spudger, plastic removal tool or  tweezers work best to remove the connectors from the receiving board. The cables connecting to the receiving board will be secured with a light adhesive that can be easily scraped away to remove the desired connector.</has_title>
  <has_order rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5</has_order>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="#Step_130697">
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#Step"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/FiNrsRVrQetyIKmP.standard"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/YWSdGH1gkhdIEYdu.standard"/>
  <has_title rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Motor direction and propeller direction is crucial to proper flight, refer to this format. The ++Front Left++ motor identified by Red/Blue wires is a '''Standard'''(''Clockwise'') spinning motor, it pairs with a ''White'' '''A2''' propeller. The ++Front Right++ motor identified by the Black/White wires is a '''Reverse'''(''CounterClockwise'') spinning motor, it pairs with a  ''White'' '''B2 '''propeller. The ++Back Left++ motor identified by the Black/White wires is a '''Reverse'''(''CounterClockwise'') spinning motor, it pairs with a ''Black'' '''B1''' propeller. The ++Back Right++ motor identified by the Red/Blue wires is a '''Standard'''(''Clockwise'') spinning motor, it pairs with a ''Black'' '''A1''' propeller.</has_title>
  <has_order rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">6</has_order>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="#Step_130698">
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#Step"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/drcjatgRsiN6PMYx.standard"/>
  <has_title rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The wiring can appear confusing, don't be alarmed. It's quite easy, and absolutely necessary for proper flight. The 4 bottom connectors receive the motor power cables that are Red/Yellow wire pairs from each motor. Counting the sockets 1-4 from left to right. Front Right motor connects to socket 1. Front Left motor connects to socket 2. Rear Left motor connects to socket 3. Rear Right motor connects to socket 4. The Red/Green wire pairs from each motor power the LED to that branch, they connect to 4 of 6 sockets on the top of the receiving board. There is no sequence required. The headlight LED, and underside LED bars connect to the remaining 2 sockets on the top of the receiving board. Again, no sequence is required.  All LED Red/Black and Red/Green wire pairs connect to top of the receiving board.</has_title>
  <has_order rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">7</has_order>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="#Tool_metal_spudger_set">
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#Tool"/>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="#Step_130572">
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#Step"/>
  <has_tool rdf:resource="#Tool_tweezers"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/4XKSuyF4VlsV5tgc.standard"/>
  <has_title rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">After having the arm separate from the rest of the drone, take a pair of tweezers and remove the silver nut from the bottom of the carbon tube.</has_title>
  <has_order rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">8</has_order>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="#Step_130693">
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#Step"/>
  <has_tool rdf:resource="#Tool_tweezers"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/bMxM4bFoo5irNytx.standard"/>
  <has_title rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Once the ball-bearing is removed, use tweezers and remove the entire tube and gear from the drone arm assembly.</has_title>
  <has_order rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">9</has_order>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="#Step_130694">
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#Step"/>
  <has_tool rdf:resource="#Tool_metal_spudger_set"/>
  <has_tool rdf:resource="#Tool_tweezers"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/YYRSUwNju2JFSjRQ.standard"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/IxxMDxO6kuvsdKgh.standard"/>
  <has_title rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">If the tooth gear attached to your carbon tube is the only gear damaged/stripped, then you need not continue. However, if your smaller gear is damaged/stripped as well, then you will need lightly pry it free from the metal post it sits on.  A spudger or flat tip screwdriver will work. Once it is free, take your tweezers and remove it completely from the drone arm assembly.</has_title>
  <has_order rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">10</has_order>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="#Step_130685">
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#Step"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/aRITCkcvfUxxTInJ.standard"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/DjnxL2SmwdgQNfnd.standard"/>
  <has_title rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">When you are done, find the cable which connects camera to the receiving board of the drone. After finding it, unplug cable from chip using a metal spudger.</has_title>
  <has_order rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">8</has_order>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="#Step_130686">
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#Step"/>
  <has_tool rdf:resource="#Tool_ph000_phillips_screwdriver"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/aIrxBLSABDYGZbiU.standard"/>
  <has_image rdf:resource="#https://d3nevzfk7ii3be.cloudfront.net/igi/ZiZvoTDhwlGlnoO4.standard"/>
  <has_title rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Last step is to take out all 4 (5mm) screws from the main frame where camera is fixed using a PH #000 screwdriver. After all four screws are removed from each corner of the camera apparatus, separate it from the main frame.</has_title>
  <has_order rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">9</has_order>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

